<div class="zp-post">

<p>Read these two sentences out loud: “I was hungry. I made a sandwich.” Both are correct, but together they sound like a robot wrote them. Now read this: “I was hungry, so I made a sandwich.” Same facts, same words, and it sounds like a person.</p>
<p>That small jump is what sentence combining teaches. You take short, choppy sentences and join them so the reader can feel how the ideas connect. It’s a skill that improves your essays, your emails, your exam answers and your speaking, and the only way to build it is practice.</p>
<p>This page gives you 20 sentence combining exercises in five groups, with a full answer key and a short reason for every answer. You’ll also get a three-word routine, a connector cheat sheet and a self-check list. I’ve taught English grammar for 22 years, and the students who improve fastest are the ones who try the exercise before reading the answer. If you want the method behind these exercises first, read my guide on <a href="https://bkandekar.github.io/ZeroErrorEnglishPro/blog/combine-sentences-using-clauses-sentence-improvement/">how to combine two sentences into one</a>.</p>
